+\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{A thread of Ariadne for SGA 4, SGA \texorpdfstring{$4\frac12$}{4½} and SGA 5}
+\begin{center}
+\textbf{A thread of Ariadne for SGA 4, SGA $4\frac12$ and SGA 5.}
+\end{center}
+\label{Ariane}
+
+Exposés I to VI of SGA 4 give the general theory of Grothendieck topologies.
+Very detailed, they can be valuable when studying exotic topologies, such as
+the one that gives rise to crystalline cohomology. For the étale topology,
+so close to classical intuition, such an imposing safeguard is unnecessary:
+it suffices to know (for example) Godement's book [4], and to have a little
+faith. Other possible references are Chapters I to III of Artin's notes [1],
+Giraud's Bourbaki exposé [3], or \hyperref[I:1]{[Arcata] I}. Exposés VII and
+VIII begin the study of the étale topology. They are more detailed than
+Chapter III of Artin and \hyperref[I:2]{[Arcata] II}.
+
+The study of curves is the key to étale cohomology. Artin begins it in
+Exposé IX of SGA 4; consequences concerning cohomological dimension are
+given in X. The essential points are taken up in \hyperref[I:3]{[Arcata] III}.
+Exposé XI is unnecessary for what follows; it contains an elegant proof of
+the comparison theorem between étale cohomology and classical cohomology
+in the special case of smooth complex algebraic varieties.
+
+Exposés XII and XIII prove the fundamental base change theorem for a proper
+morphism. As Artin observed in (algebraic approximation of structures over
+complete local rings, Publ. Math. IHES 36 (1969), pp. 23--58--§3), his
+approximation theorem makes it possible to simplify the proof. This is the
+route followed in \hyperref[I:4]{[Arcata] IV}, where the many dévissages in
+the proof are only sketched, and the applications in Exposé XIV are indicated
+only very briefly. The theorem makes it possible to define ``cohomology with
+compact supports'' and the ``higher direct image with proper supports''
+functors $R^i f_!$ (whose properties reduce to those of the higher direct image
+functors for a proper morphism). This is the subject of the prolix Exposé XVII;
+the essentials are given in \hyperref[I:4]{[Arcata] IV}, but portions of XVII
+may be independently useful.
+
+\PrintPage{2}
+Exposés XV and XVI centre on the fundamental local acyclicity theorem for
+smooth morphisms. The influence of SGA 7 shows through in the less detailed
+parallel exposé \hyperref[I:5]{[Arcata] V}, which also contains the proof of
+the results needed from SGA 2.
+
+The exposé \hyperref[I:6]{[Arcata] VI} is devoted to Poincaré duality; it is
+clearer than SGA 4 XVIII but does not claim to give a complete proof. For those
+who insist on a purely algebraic proof, it is simplified by a reference to
+\hyperref[V]{[Duality]} §3; it does not contain the proof of the formalism
+obeyed by the trace morphism (XVIII §2).
+
+In étale cohomology there is a duality formalism analogous to that of coherent
+cohomology. To establish it, Grothendieck used resolution of singularities
+and the purity conjecture (for the statement, see \hyperref[IV]{[Cycle]}
+2.1.4), established in a relative setting in SGA 4 XVI and---modulo
+resolution---in equal characteristic in SGA 4 XIX. The key points are
+established by another method in \hyperref[VII]{[Finiteness th.]}, for schemes
+of finite type over a regular scheme of dimension 0 or 1. Various developments
+are given in SGA 5 I. In SGA 5 III it is shown how this formalism implies the
+very general Lefschetz--Verdier trace formula.
+
+We see that in the original version of SGA 5 the Lefschetz--Verdier formula
+was established only conjecturally. Moreover, its local terms were not
+calculated. For the application to $L$-functions, this seminar contains
+another proof, this one complete, in the special case of the Frobenius
+morphism. This is the one that appears in \hyperref[II]{[Report]}. Other
+references: for the statement and the outline of the dévissages,
+Grothendieck's Bourbaki exposé [5]; for a brief description of the reduction
+(due to Grothendieck) of the crucial case to a case already treated by Weil,
+[2] §10; for an $\ell$-adic treatment of this last case,
+\hyperref[IV]{[Cycle]} §3.
+
+SGA 5 also contains a detailed treatment of the passage from
+$\mathbb Z/\ell^n$-cohomology to $\mathbb Z_\ell$-cohomology (Exposés V and
+VI, by J.P. Jouanolou), and of the formalism of the Frobenius morphism
+(Exposé XV, by C. Houzel). Exposés X
+\PrintPage{3}
+and XIII, written by I. Bucur, calculate the Euler--Poincaré characteristic
+of a sheaf on a curve and the trace of the endomorphism of its cohomology
+defined by certain correspondences. In VII, Jouanolou calculates the
+cohomology of classical schemes and gives applications. Furthermore, Serre's
+exposé ``Introduction à la théorie de Brauer'' was included in his book [6]
+(Part 3).
+
